WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.560
Hearing loss, a white forelock, and a stretch of gut with no nerve cells.

2
00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:09.920
Why would one gene cause all three?

3
00:00:10.933 --> 00:00:15.653
Some patients have Hirschsprung disease and Waardenburg syndrome together, from one

4
00:00:15.653 --> 00:00:18.053
mutant gene. Hold that puzzle.

5
00:00:19.083 --> 00:00:23.063
Early on, the ectoderm splits into neural plate and future skin.

6
00:00:23.943 --> 00:00:26.103
The neural crest forms at the border between them.

7
00:00:27.033 --> 00:00:31.633
When the plate rolls up into the neural tube, the border cells end up along its top.

8
00:00:32.633 --> 00:00:36.633
In the chick trunk, they then let go of their neighbours, break through the basement

9
00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:38.033
membrane, and crawl away.

10
00:00:38.973 --> 00:00:41.753
In the mouse head, many leave before the folds have closed.

11
00:00:42.767 --> 00:00:47.907
This change, from a cell in a sheet to a free cell, is an epithelial-to-mesenchymal

12
00:00:47.907 --> 00:00:48.907
transition,

13
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:51.640
What they become differs along the body.

14
00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:56.710
Cranial crest makes cartilage and bone of the face and much of the skull.

15
00:00:57.733 --> 00:01:01.813
Vagal and sacral crest make the neurons and glia of the gut wall.

16
00:01:02.817 --> 00:01:07.977
Trunk crest makes sensory and sympathetic neurons, adrenal medulla, Schwann cells

17
00:01:08.197 --> 00:01:10.057
and melanocytes, the pigment cells.

18
00:01:11.117 --> 00:01:13.337
Here is the one idea to carry through.

19
00:01:14.077 --> 00:01:15.697
One border, many destinations.

20
00:01:16.867 --> 00:01:21.047
Where along the body a crest population starts biases what it can become.

21
00:01:22.067 --> 00:01:26.207
The signals on its route, and where it stops, help decide which fates appear.

22
00:01:27.217 --> 00:01:28.777
This holds for populations.

23
00:01:29.837 --> 00:01:32.557
Some single cells are already biased before they leave.

24
00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:36.910
Many tissues share that start, and many need the same genes.

25
00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:40.950
So one failed gene can strike tissues that look unrelated.

26
00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:47.900
Predict. If gut neurons and pigment cells share a start, what could one broken gene

27
00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:48.900
do?

28
00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:58.030
It could fail both lineages at once, if both need that gene.

29
00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:00.190
Chapter three names the genes.

30
00:02:01.283 --> 00:02:06.043
The takeaway. The start biases the options, and the journey helps choose.
